<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!-- LatestMajor, not LatestMinor: type providers erase to the analyzed
         project's RUNTIME references, and a net8 host handed lib/net10.0
         SQLProvider assemblies cannot instantiate them ("SqlDataProvider is
         not defined" on a project that builds cleanly). Running on the
         newest installed runtime lets the tool typecheck projects that
         target it; machines with only net8 still run on net8. -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
    <!-- ships as a dotnet tool: dotnet tool install fsharp-refactor -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>fsharp-refactor</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>fsharp-refactor</PackageId>
    <Description>Applies the FSharp.Refactor analyzers' quick fixes to F# code from the command line. Point it at a project, a script, a solution, a directory or a glob; --dry-run reports without editing. The tool clarifies the (agent-generated or old) code's intent, reduces non-alphanumeric noise, and uses built-in .NET libraries more efficiently.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.8.35: From a run over a 370-file Fable codebase (GitHub issues #1 to #6). FR0147 no longer opens a namespace that an `open` would also reach through a partial path: F# resolves `open X` against every namespace already open - `Microsoft.FSharp` always, and each open of the file - and opens every match, so `open Core` for a user namespace `Core` also opened Microsoft.FSharp.Core, re-opened its Operators over a user&apos;s own `tan`, and failed with FS0893, an error; `open IO` under `open System` is the same. Such a namespace is now a note naming the second one. FR0162 stays quiet on a slot that is reset - a store of `None`, `null` or another empty value anywhere, an in-flight task dropped once it settles so the next call retries, which a `Lazy` never could - and no longer counts a store inside a lambda under the emptiness test as guarded, since the lambda runs later. FR0035 leaves a module-level literal of fewer than eight written-out elements alone, and converts one of fewer than sixteen through the HashSet companion rather than in place to an F# Set: measured on .NET 10, a HashSet beats the list scan from about eight elements, while the Set, a comparison tree, catches up only at about twelve when every probe misses and about twenty when half hit (strings, at eight: list 29 ms, HashSet 22, Set 31 all missing; 34, 28 and 49 half hitting). The old &quot;2.5x even at five elements&quot; did not reproduce. `{ &quot;FR0035&quot;: { &quot;minElements&quot;: 8, &quot;setMinElements&quot;: 16 } }` moves the floors. Fable projects, those referencing Fable.Core: FR0130 takes only private and internal values, `--api-changes` or not, because Fable exports every public module value and drops a `[&lt;Literal&gt;]` from its JavaScript, Python, Dart and Rust output alike. FR0038 skips its culture note, since every Fable target compares strings ordinally. A project bound to JavaScript - a Fable.Browser.*, Fable.Node, Fable.Deno or Fable.Promise reference - gets no FR0162 note, one thread racing nothing, and an FR0055 note without the cancellation claim; Fable.Core alone keeps both, since the Rust and Python targets have threads. A project bound to a browser gets no FR0121 note for `DateTime.Today` or `DateTime.Now`, the user&apos;s own clock, while `DateTime.UtcNow.Date` keeps its note. FR0070 and FR0059 stay on: the Rust target stores a `[&lt;Struct&gt;]` record by value and a reference record behind a counted pointer. The tool: `--codes` and `--categories` keep the analyzers of the other rules from running, rather than running them for messages the run then discarded - a `--categories correctness` run spent minutes a pass in FR0147, an idiom rule. A pass after the first sweeps only the files the previous pass changed and those compiled after the first of them, so the last pass, the one that finds nothing, is no longer a full sweep; a rule reading a LATER file may find new work in an earlier one only on the next run. FR0006&apos;s active patterns for one match all go in in the same pass: they share one insertion point, which the tool took one per pass until its re-fire guard tripped on three guards. The output prints one line per finding, with its edit count when it has several, and each pass totals &quot;N edit(s) applied, from M finding(s)&quot;, so the count matches the report&apos;s findings. FR0015 names a hoisted regex after the binding it serves - `isPostcode` gives `postcodeRegex`, `collapseSpaces` gives `collapseSpacesRegex` - and, where that name is taken, to the function&apos;s whole name (`isPostcodeRegex`) before the pattern-derived one; a pattern already hoisted to a binding above, in the same module, is reused rather than built a second time, and one pattern hoisted from two sites in one pass becomes one binding the second site takes over on the next pass. FR0164 counts only a `let mutable` whose scope encloses the loop&apos;s condition: one in another function, of the same name, downgraded the RemoveAll fix to a snapshot. FR0168&apos;s success binder is `parsed`, or another name the file does not spell, never a `v` shadowing one in scope, and a try after `else`, `then`, `-&gt;` or `=` with nothing after it moves to lines of its own - only where that keyword&apos;s construct opens the line (an arm&apos;s `| pat -&gt;`, an `if`, `elif` or `else`, a `let`, `use` or member binding) and no lambda sits on it, since `let x = if c then try` would leave the match offside of its `if` and a lambda&apos;s body would leave the lambda. A Parse argument that is an unannotated parameter (`let f s =`, `fun s -&gt;`) is written `(s: string)` in the TryParse call: `Parse` resolved its one-argument overload before `s` had a type, and the several of `TryParse` could not. FR0013 stays quiet where `.editorconfig` tells Fantomas `fsharp_space_before_lowercase_invocation = false`, the repository&apos;s way of saying it writes `f(x)`, unless `--codes` or fsharprefactor.json names the rule. From an independent review and the dogfood run of this version. The analyzers&apos; deep-stack workers now run each job under the execution context of the code that queued it: a worker is a long-lived thread, and it had kept the context of whichever run created it, so the first `--codes` run in a process switched those rules off for every later run in it - the tests, the corpus harness and `--mcp` call the tool in-process. FR0015 reuses a hoisted binding only where the site&apos;s declaration does not spell its name (a parameter or local of that name would take the call - a string does not compile, a Regex tests another pattern) and the binding is under the same `#if` as the site; a function&apos;s second and third regex take its name numbered (`parseRegex2`) in the same pass, where they had been dropped as collisions and returned a pass later under the pattern&apos;s letters; and its hoists at one point go in together, in the order they were found, as FR0006&apos;s do.

 0.8.34: Silent sweep rewrites closed, each shown by a failing test before its guard went in - and every guard built the same way round: the fix by default, a note only where the analysis positively finds the edge, never for want of a proof. FR0172 names a tail read beside the second element `h :: (s :: _ as t)`: `h :: s :: t` bound the rest after the second, one short of `.Tail`, and typechecked; and it declines an arm whose body starts on the `-&gt;` line and continues below, a line right under a lengthening edit indented past it, a line aligned exactly to a token a shortening edit moves, or a single-line arm with a line below anchored to text after it, since the moved text would push those lines offside (FS0058) or read them as arguments, with no compile error to stop it. FR0168 keeps the try where the Parse argument is a shape that throws on its own - an index (`args.[1]`), a cut or a parse of its own (`s.Substring 5`, `Convert.ToInt32 x`), a numeric conversion (`int x`, `Checked.int x`), a `.Value`/`.Head` read anywhere in the chain (`o.Value.Trim()`) of an option, a Nullable, a Lazy or a list - never a KeyValuePair&apos;s or a record&apos;s - or a downcast (`o :?> string`), or a partial function of FSharp.Core (`List.head`, `Option.get`, `Map.find`, `Map.pick`) - since the catch answered that exception with the fallback too; a getter, a user call, a `Trim` is taken as a read and the fix stands; `TimeSpan` joins the types whose Parse overflows. FR0170 keeps the loop only on what it sees write the dictionary before a read: the receiver handed on, reassigned or rebound, a store through a value of this file bound to it - by name, a tuple pattern or a record field - or through a setter of this file (a store runs the setter, a read the getter), a function, member, getter, active pattern or forced `seq`/`async`/`lazy` value defined in this file whose body touches a dictionary (a `let mutable` hook by every body assigned to it in this file; an auto-property is a dictionary, a read through `ContainsKey` or `Count` is no touch), and in a `seq { }` or `task { }` each `yield`, `let!` and `do!` (an else-less `if` or a loop with a value is an implicit yield, a unit statement is not) - a getter counted where it is read, argument or not, every loop nested around a read counted whole, a read inside a local function keeping the loop, and a receiver segment whose getter of this file builds a dictionary, itself or through a helper of this file, too. A `log k`, an interface call, a delegate, an event, a helper defined in another file, LINQ, a field store, a write to another dictionary: the fix, since nothing visible says otherwise; the accepted residual is user code behind such a call writing the very dictionary the loop walks. FR0060&apos;s eager-filter probe takes a dotted read as total unless a segment is a member known to throw - `.Value` of an option, a Nullable or a Lazy, `.Head`/`.Tail` of a list, an indexer, `.Force()`, a Memory&apos;s `.Span` - so `s.Length` and a user getter fire as FSharpLint&apos;s own hint does. FR0166&apos;s length guard proves the cut for an immutable value (never a byref) and for a `let mutable` not written between the guard and the cut - a local one by a `&lt;-` or a `&amp;` there, so the `while not (isNull line)` reader loop stays exact; a module or class one also by a call there whose body, defined in this file, assigns it - itself, through the calls it makes, or by a `&amp;` to it, whatever module path the receiver reaches it by, and never another mutable of that name in another scope; the rest of an `if` condition after the guard and a loop between count as between - qualified by modules or read through immutable fields, get-only `member val`s and BCL getters. FR0167 writes `FSharp.Core.Operators.nonNull` and FR0173 `FSharp.Core.Operators.max 0 n`, which no `nonNull`, `max` or module named `Operators` of the project&apos;s own can take. FR0164&apos;s RemoveAll fix gives way to the snapshot for a condition reading a `let mutable` or a Span, or a loop holding a comment, and its editor offers pass the comment guard. The analyzers no longer hold a thread-pool thread while a deep-stack worker runs a rule. Each queued analyzer blocked one, and on a large project - the dogfood step over the analyzers project itself - every pool thread ended up waiting on a worker while the worker waited on the thread pool for the FCS parse of the default hints behind a lazy: a deadlock that never finished. The analyzer now awaits its worker instead.

 0.8.33: The analyzers no longer walk the whole file once per candidate. About thirty rules asked which expressions sit inside a range by testing every expression of the file for each hit, a cost that grew with the square of the file: on an 18k-line file SeqEnumeratedTwice (FR0169) took 5.7 s, FailwithContext (FR0092) 6.6 s, SwallowedException (FR0055) 4.3 s and Accumulation (FR0050/FR0107) 3.5 s of their own work; they now take 9 to 43 ms, UseBinding and AccumulatorLoop (FR0156) about 0.7 s. The shared pieces: `AstIndex.exprsWithin`/`patsWithin`, a range query over a per-file start order that returns exactly the nodes a walk finds, in its order; `AstIndex.mentionsOf`, where each name is read; per-file tables behind the helpers FR0107, the Option/Result wrappers and FR0012 share; and `OptionModule.symbolUseAt`, a per-file memo of FCS&apos;s `GetSymbolUseAtLocation` that every typed lookup of the rules goes through. Every message and fix is unchanged - byte-identical on synthetic 4.5k- and 18k-line files, identical dry-run reports on five real projects - and a dry-run sweep of the analyzers project went from 384 s to 229 s, of the tool project from 89 s to 35 s. The analyzers&apos; own code takes its performance notes: single-character `StartsWith`/`EndsWith` use the char overload, two recursive `seq { yield! }` walks became one, a list&apos;s length is read once outside its loop. The api pass no longer takes a build-order-only `ProjectReference` (`ReferenceOutputAssembly` false) for a consumer, unless the project also references the built dll directly: every pass over this repository&apos;s own Ionide twin loaded the Analyzers project and both test projects behind it only to report each &quot;cannot be read&quot;. The end-to-end tests that build a synthetic solution carry `Category=Slow`, so `dotnet test --filter &quot;Category!=Slow&quot;` leaves them out.

 0.8.32: FR0173 takes a mapper spanning lines only as a lambda whose body starts on a line of its own. A `match` or a first statement after the arrow moves with that line: `(fun i -&gt; match i with` over arms aligned to the `match` fell offside (FS0058) when the count&apos;s `max 0` made the line longer, and a second statement aligned to a first one became its argument when the line got shorter - `tap i` over `i` read as `tap i i`, a changed result with no compile error to stop it.

 0.8.31: FR0035 converts a list whose element can carry a float to a `Set` only when every element is a written-out non-NaN constant, since the Set finds a NaN `List.contains` never did. FR0167&apos;s String twins go through `nonNull` on FSharp.Core 9+, which throws on a null string as the copy did, so a sweep applies them. FR0041 sweeps `Array.contains v arr` on `int[]`/`int64[]` to `Enumerable.Contains(arr, v)`, exact on those types; the aggregations stay notes. FR0174 sweeps a nullable column of an exact type compared by `=`/`&lt;`/`&lt;=`/`&gt;`/`&gt;=` with a non-null value outside any `not`, where SQL and .NET both drop the NULL row. FR0071 no longer hoists a division by a signed integral `-1` (`MinValue / -1` overflows) or anything in a file under `open Checked`.

 0.8.30: FR0174 (performance, fix), CR0178's twin: in C#-style LINQ over `open System.Linq`, a query copied before `Where`/`Select` loads every row - `db.Orders.ToList().Where(fun o -&gt; o.Total &gt; 0).Select(fun o -&gt; o.Id)` becomes `db.Orders.Where(fun o -&gt; o.Total &gt; 0).Select(fun o -&gt; o.Id).ToList()`, for value-column comparisons and projections only, and by a sweep only where the chain's new List type binds as the IEnumerable did; the pipeline shape is behind the default-off `pipelines` knob. A review of the whole code closed some eighty defects, each reproduced by a failing test: non-UTF-8 files were rewritten with U+FFFD (now read in the system code page and put back byte for byte), the verification build stopped at its first failure without building consumers, a timeout left earlier passes' fixes unverified, and silent rewrites in FR0166, FR0010, FR0035, FR0021, FR0006, FR0170, FR0060, FR0075, FR0004, FR0005, FR0119, FR0049, FR0118, FR0160, FR0071, FR0026, FR0053, FR0023, FR0029, FR0034 and FR0157 (record-field rewrites now editor-only) got guards; FR0049 reads `winner = work &amp;&amp; work.IsCompleted &amp;&amp; work.Result` as a read; a C# consumer's escalated analyzer error (CA/IDE) counts as a code error.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <Authors>Tuomas Hietanen</Authors>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>fsharp;analyzers;refactoring;codefix;dotnet-tool</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Unlicense</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="FileWalk.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Workspace.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Update="FSharp.Core" Version="10.1.201" />
    <!-- FSharp.Analyzers.SDK drags in 6.0.0, which is OLDER than the 8.0.0.0 a
         SQLProvider type provider asks for: its MySqlConnector fails to
         initialise against ours and the whole project reports as broken.
         Pinned forward so the provider's own reference resolves. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="8.0.0" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
